    "reading": {
        "body": "South Carolina's data center inventory is dominated by a single operator in a single location: Google accounts for nine of the fourteen facilities recorded, all clustered within a tight radius in Berkeley County.\n\nThe geographic concentration is striking. The nine Google entries share coordinates close enough that they represent one campus recorded as multiple distinct structures rather than nine independent sites. That campus pattern is typical of hyperscale build-out, where a single power and land agreement produces several adjacent buildings over successive construction phases. The four DartPoints facilities are distributed across the state's larger metros, and one entry, the DC BLOX cable landing station in Myrtle Beach, marks a point where subsea or long-haul fiber meets land, which matters for latency and connectivity regardless of local compute capacity.\n\nThe total recorded capacity figure is 2 MW, attributed entirely to the DartPoints Columbia facility. No power figures appear for any of the nine Google buildings, which almost certainly account for the large majority of actual load on the South Carolina grid. The data as given therefore understates real consumption substantially and cannot support any conclusion about the state's total data center power draw or its relationship to grid capacity.",
